[17592186549196 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549219 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549196 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549196 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + 2 y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549196 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fa7fa7e-c30f-5aad-b305-af6fc97a49df" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549196 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}9\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =9{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549225 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549198 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + y^{2} + 1$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-2\\le y\\le 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "161eeef3-7763-576b-af4d-53c8662ff260" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549198 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2} + 1)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y^{2} + 1)=2 y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\left(x + y^{2} + 1\\right)-x+y^{2}}}\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(2 y\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{4 y^{2} + 2}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{2\\left(2 y^{2} + 1\\right)}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}}\\sqrt{2}\\cancel{\\sqrt{2 y^{2} + 1}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}4 \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(-2\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(4\\right)\\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549208 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549199 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549208 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549208 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + 2 y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70de3dca-c539-516f-9ac7-21707e474aee" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549208 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + 2 y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3 \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\left(1-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{6}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6 \\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549210 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549211 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549211 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x + y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549211 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "940d874d-7f17-585c-aaa1-3e37ae6a49cf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549211 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =2 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549224 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549227 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549224 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549224 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2 x + 2 y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549224 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d486a075-a82c-5df5-890f-0518c34dd229" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549224 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x + 2 y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x + 2 y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}1\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}1\\sqrt{1+\\left(2\\right)^{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\text{Area}(D)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\cdot\\left(2-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =3\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)\\\\ & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549230 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186105887.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186549230 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "7cad3fa8-ad87-5af1-ba2f-90c3e5aa35ef" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559992 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559993 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/013cdd1ba73ee79ef51d00b7dc9f48488cee2299eb593accadfbf026981169c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "141fe36a-507a-57e3-9528-7652085c824c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559993 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{32}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{32 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560024 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559998 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/79/79b09a881e6809e097e982b58d81a037d162f6b5820894e172955df1551415ca.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c380e3c-eb26-50bd-a669-b181f14f84c3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186559998 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{1}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560005 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560035 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560005 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560005 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/137a452f1b378359fdd974b11781dddf1e7e9b469d864699b044eaf1cc1a7c20.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560005 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d921d71-b4a3-5244-8d78-9db05b10437e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560005 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the s