sed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{4}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560050 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186560045 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/44fada7635b47f3f7ef635b0466f21d87c71b52e7f39fddb5d36c16472f4ce82.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the second quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." #ed